---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import PageNav from '../../components/PageNav.astro';
import { t } from '../../i18n';
import type { Locale } from '../../i18n/config';

const locale: Locale = 'en';
---

<DocsLayout>
  <h1>{t(locale, 'nav.keyTransfer')}</h1>
  <p class="lead">Transfer the customer's E2EE keypair from the PWA to a native app on the same device.</p>

  <h2>Problem</h2>
  <p>The customer's private key lives in the PWA's IndexedDB. When the user installs the native app, the app has no key and would generate a fresh one, making every message wrapped to the old key undecryptable.</p>
  <p>There is <strong>no direct channel</strong> between the PWA and the app. The payload must travel through the backend, encrypted with a secret the backend never sees.</p>

  <h2>Security Model</h2>
  <p>The security property is <strong>channel separation</strong>:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The ciphertext travels over HTTPS (backend sees it but cannot decrypt).</li>
    <li>The PIN travels through the user's eyes and fingers (never sent to the server).</li>
  </ul>

  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Party</th><th>Sees</th><th>Does not see</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Backend / DB</td><td>transferId, ciphertext, iv, salt</td><td>PIN, plaintext keypair</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Network attacker</td><td>Same as backend</td><td>PIN</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Another app</td><td>Nothing</td><td>PIN</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Cryptographic Parameters</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Purpose</th><th>Algorithm</th><th>Parameters</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>PIN → KEK</td><td>PBKDF2-SHA256</td><td>200,000 iterations, 16-byte random salt</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Payload encryption</td><td>AES-256-GCM</td><td>256-bit key, 96-bit IV, 128-bit tag</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>PIN Protection</h2>
  <p>A 6-digit PIN has ~20 bits of entropy. It is safe because of:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Short TTL</strong> (5 minutes) on the Redis entry.</li>
    <li><strong>Attempt limiting</strong> (5 attempts, then the entry is destroyed).</li>
    <li><strong>One-shot retrieval</strong> (GET transitions the entry to <code>RETRIEVED</code>).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Flow</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Source client (PWA) reads keypair from IndexedDB.</li>
    <li>Payload is JSON → deflate → AES-256-GCM encrypted with KEK derived from PIN.</li>
    <li>PIN is generated locally, <strong>never sent to the server</strong>.</li>
    <li>Encrypted payload is uploaded: <code>POST /api/key-transfer</code>.</li>
    <li>Backend returns <code>transferId</code> and <code>expiresAt</code>.</li>
    <li>PWA shows PIN + store link to the user.</li>
    <li>User installs and opens the app, enters the PIN.</li>
    <li>App fetches: <code>GET /api/key-transfer/{"{transferId}"}</code>.</li>
    <li>App derives KEK, decrypts, validates, stores in secure storage.</li>
    <li>App confirms: <code>POST /api/key-transfer/{"{transferId}"}/confirm</code>.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Quick Transfer (in-conversation)</h2>
  <p>When a customer opens a conversation on a new device and cannot decrypt the history, the chat page offers to import the key from another device where the same conversation is open. The <code>transferId</code> is exchanged over the shared Socket.io connection instead of a link, so the user only reads the PIN on one screen and types it on the other.</p>
  <p>The events are routed through the <strong>customer key room</strong> (<code>customer:{'{'}customerPublicKey{'}'}</code>), so a device only receives events for its own keypair.</p>

  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Direction</th><th>Event</th><th>Payload</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>target → server</td><td><code>request_key_transfer</code></td><td><code>{'{ token }'}</code> — deeplink token of the conversation</td></tr>
      <tr><td>server → source</td><td><code>show_key_transfer_dialog</code></td><td>—</td></tr>
      <tr><td>source → server</td><td><code>send_transfer_id</code></td><td><code>{'{ transferId }'}</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>server → target</td><td><code>receive_transfer_id</code></td><td><code>{'{ transferId, conversationToken? }'}</code></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p><code>send_transfer_id</code> carries only the <code>transferId</code>: the backend resolves the conversation the transfer is bound to and routes the event to that conversation's key room. The binding is set at creation time from the optional <code>conversationToken</code> in <code>POST /api/key-transfer</code>, which the backend resolves to the conversation id <strong>server-side</strong> — the client cannot claim an arbitrary conversation id. The token is echoed back on retrieval so the target can return to the conversation after importing the key.</p>

  <h2>Payload Format</h2>
  <CodeBlock language="json" code={`{
  "v": 1,
  "keyId": "a3f9...",
  "publicKeySpki": "MIIBIjANBgkq...",
  "privateKeyPkcs8": "MIIEvQIBADANBgkq..."
}`} title="Key payload" />

  <h2>Backend Envelope</h2>
  <CodeBlock language="json" code={`{
  "ciphertext": "8f3a...",
  "iv": "9c2b...",
  "salt": "1d4e...",
  "kdf": { "alg": "PBKDF2-SHA256", "iterations": 200000 },
  "conversationToken": "d3f1..."
}`} title="Stored envelope" />
  <p><code>conversationToken</code> is optional and only present when the transfer was started from a conversation screen. It is the conversation's deeplink token — the same value already present in the conversation URL, so it is not a secret.</p>

  <PageNav
    prev={{ titleKey: 'nav.presence', path: 'presence' }}
    next={{ titleKey: 'nav.auth', path: 'auth' }}
  />
</DocsLayout>
